{%- comment -%}
  PawLunova buy box — companion parts.

  These four live here rather than in pl-buybox.liquid for one mechanical
  reason: Liquid will not let a snippet render itself, and the parts below are
  emitted from inside pl-buybox's own `anchor` and `compare` cases. Splitting
  the file is what lets those mount points carry extra content without anyone
  having to add a theme block for each one.

  That matters because templates/product.json is store-owned. Every part here
  reaches the page through a block that already exists, so nothing in this
  change depends on editing the product template.

  Called as:
      {% render 'pl-buybox-extra', part: 'ladder', p: p, v: v %}

  Parts
    ladder     every size, priced, co-present, linking to its variant
    whyprice   the reason-why that sits on the price
    signs      cited self-identification block for orthopaedic beds
    proof      checkable credibility for a shop with no reviews yet
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign p = p | default: product | default: closest.product
  assign v = v | default: p.selected_or_first_available_variant
  assign part = part | default: 'ladder'
-%}

{%- case part -%}

{%- comment -%} ---------- the size ladder, all rungs priced and co-present ----------

  The prices on this shop are already right; they are just never seen together.
  A dropdown reveals one price at a time, so Large reads as "£30 more than the
  one I was looking at". Rendered as a list, the same three numbers read as a
  range with a top and a bottom, and the middle rung inherits the compromise
  effect (Simonson & Tversky) for free.

  Each rung is a real link to that variant, so it works with JavaScript off and
  Shopify's own ?variant= handling does the selecting. It decorates the native
  picker, it does not replace it — the picker above is still what submits.

  The fit line comes from a per-variant metafield and is simply absent until
  someone fills it in. It is never generated from the size letter: "Large" is
  decorative, the centimetre is load-bearing.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- when 'ladder' -%}
  {%- unless p.has_only_default_variant -%}
    {%- if p.variants.size > 1 -%}
      <div class="pl-bb pl-bb-ladder">
        {%- if title != blank -%}<p class="pl-bb-ladder__title">{{ title }}</p>{%- endif -%}
        <ul class="pl-bb-ladder__list">
          {%- for var in p.variants -%}
            {%- assign fit = var.metafields.custom.fit_note -%}
            <li class="pl-bb-ladder__row{% if var.id == v.id %} is-on{% endif %}{% unless var.available %} is-out{% endunless %}">
              <a href="{{ p.url }}?variant={{ var.id }}"{% if var.id == v.id %} aria-current="true"{% endif %}>
                <span class="pl-bb-ladder__name">{{ var.title }}</span>
                {%- if fit != blank -%}<span class="pl-bb-ladder__fit">{{ fit }}</span>{%- endif -%}
                <span class="pl-bb-ladder__price">
                  {{ var.price | money }}{% unless var.available %} <em>sold out</em>{% endunless %}
                </span>
              </a>
            </li>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </ul>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endunless -%}

{%- comment -%} ---------- the reason-why, sitting on the price ----------

  A price with a stated reason is accepted where a bare price is argued with.

  Note what this does NOT do: it makes no claim about what a cheap bed is made
  of or how fast it fails, because this shop cannot evidence that and a
  regulator would ask it to. It states only its own two numbers and then hands
  the shopper the question to ask of anything they are comparing against. The
  comparison still happens; we simply do not make an unprovable assertion on a
  competitor's behalf.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- when 'whyprice' -%}
  {%- liquid
    capture mats2
      for m in p.metafields.shopify.material.value
        echo m.label
        echo ' '
      endfor
    endcapture
    assign tagstr2 = p.tags | join: ' '
    assign hay2 = mats2 | append: ' ' | append: tagstr2 | append: ' ' | append: p.type | downcase
    assign foam2 = false
    if hay2 contains 'memory foam'
      assign foam2 = true
    endif
  -%}
  {%- if foam2 -%}
    <details class="pl-bb pl-bb-why"{% if open_by_default %} open{% endif %}>
      <summary>Why {{ v.price | money_without_trailing_zeros }}, and not forty-five?</summary>
      <div class="pl-bb-why__body">
        <p>
          Because two numbers decide whether a dog bed is still a dog bed in year three,
          and this one publishes both: a <b>50kg/m&sup3; high-density core</b>, and a
          <b>five-year guarantee</b> against that core losing its shape. If it flattens
          inside those five years we replace the bed.
        </p>
        <p>
          Density is not firmness. A low-density foam can feel firm in the shop and be
          a flat mat by month six, because there was never much material in it to start
          with. That is the whole difference, and it is the only part worth paying for.
        </p>
        <p class="pl-bb-why__ask">
          So ask it of any bed you are weighing this against:
          <b>what is the foam density, and how long is it guaranteed for?</b>
          Whatever you are comparing, those two numbers are the bed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  {%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%} ---------- what your dog is telling you ----------

  The emotionally loaded block, and the one with the most rules attached.

  It opens on a real, cited, counter-intuitive finding rather than on guilt.
  That ordering is deliberate: the statistic alarms and absolves in the same
  breath ("nobody is looking at that age"), which is what makes an owner read
  on instead of closing the tab. Guilt alone makes people leave.

  Every sign listed is one a vet would recognise. Nothing here diagnoses, and
  the vet line is not a disclaimer bolted on the end — under CAP 12.1 a bed may
  not be presented as a treatment, and ASA has already upheld against a dog-bed
  advertiser (A&T International, Bella&Toby) for efficacy claims, holding that a
  customer testimonial is not evidence for an objective health claim. So this
  block claims one thing only, which is true and sufficient: it changes the
  surface they lie on.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- when 'signs' -%}
  <section class="pl-bb pl-bb-signs" aria-labelledby="pl-signs-h">
    <h2 class="pl-bb-signs__h" id="pl-signs-h">{{ heading | default: 'What your dog is telling you' }}</h2>
    <p class="pl-bb-signs__lede">{{ lede | default: 'Most owners spot it years after it starts.' }}</p>
    <p>
      In 2024 researchers at North Carolina State University X-rayed 123 dogs aged between
      eight months and four years. Almost <b>40%</b> already had osteoarthritis in at least
      one joint. Of the twenty-nine showing clinical signs of it, <b>two</b> were receiving
      anything at all for the pain. Not because their owners did not care &mdash; because at
      that age nobody is looking, and dogs adapt rather than complain.
    </p>
    <p class="pl-bb-signs__cite">
      Enomoto et al., <cite>Scientific Reports</cite> 14, 2827 (2024).
      <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-52324-9" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">Read the study</a>
    </p>
    <p class="pl-bb-signs__ask">Six things a vet would ask about. Tick the ones you have seen this month.</p>
    <ul class="pl-bb-signs__list">
      <li><label><input type="checkbox"><span>Stiff or slow to get up after a long rest</span></label></li>
      <li><label><input type="checkbox"><span>Lagging on walks where they used to lead</span></label></li>
      <li><label><input type="checkbox"><span>Hesitating at stairs, or no longer jumping onto the sofa or into the car</span></label></li>
      <li><label><input type="checkbox"><span>Restless at night &mdash; changing position again and again, or moving between spots</span></label></li>
      <li><label><input type="checkbox"><span>Licking repeatedly at one joint</span></label></li>
      <li><label><input type="checkbox"><span>Sitting or lying in a lopsided, propped-up way</span></label></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="pl-bb-signs__out">
      Any two of these is worth a conversation with your vet. It is also worth getting them
      off a hard floor tonight.
    </p>
    <p class="pl-bb-signs__vet">
      A bed is not a treatment and does not replace veterinary care. What it can change is the
      surface they spend half their life lying on.
    </p>
  </section>

{%- comment -%} ---------- proof that does not depend on reviews ----------

  This shop has no reviews yet, and the honest answer to that is not a borrowed
  star rating — it is to lead with the things that ARE checkable. Every line
  here is either a published policy or a number stated elsewhere on the site,
  and each one is a setting, so nothing renders that the merchant has not
  affirmatively stood behind.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- when 'proof' -%}
  <ul class="pl-bb pl-bb-proof">
    {%- if p1 != blank -%}<li><b>{{ p1 }}</b>{% if p1s != blank %}<span>{{ p1s }}</span>{% endif %}</li>{%- endif -%}
    {%- if p2 != blank -%}<li><b>{{ p2 }}</b>{% if p2s != blank %}<span>{{ p2s }}</span>{% endif %}</li>{%- endif -%}
    {%- if p3 != blank -%}<li><b>{{ p3 }}</b>{% if p3s != blank %}<span>{{ p3s }}</span>{% endif %}</li>{%- endif -%}
  </ul>

{%- endcase -%}
